What the matrix product is doing
\(\Sigma\) is symmetric and positive definite, so it has a symmetric square root \(\Sigma^{1/2}\), and \(\Sigma^{-1} = \Sigma^{-1/2}\Sigma^{-1/2}\). Substituting that in and regrouping:
\[
D^2 = (x-\mu)^{\!\top}\Sigma^{-1/2}\,\Sigma^{-1/2}(x-\mu)
    = \big\|\,\underbrace{\Sigma^{-1/2}(x-\mu)}_{\textstyle w}\,\big\|^2 .
\]

The Mahalanobis distance is ordinary Euclidean distance, computed after whitening. Centre by \(\mu\), apply \(\Sigma^{-1/2}\), then measure length the usual way.

The transformed vector \(w = \Sigma^{-1/2}(x - \mu)\) has mean \(0\) and covariance \(\Sigma^{-1/2}\Sigma\Sigma^{-1/2} = I\). In \(w\)-coordinates every variable has variance one and no two are correlated, so the cloud is a sphere, and Euclidean distance is finally the right tool. In practice, we use a Cholesky factor \(\Sigma = LL^{\top}\) and \(w = L^{-1}(x-\mu)\) instead of the symmetric root.
Drag the slider below to watch it happen. The dashed rings are contours of constant Mahalanobis distance at every stage, so they begin as the data’s own tilted ellipses and end as true circles. The red segment marks one fixed observation: its length on screen changes, its Mahalanobis distance does not.


